<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-glutamine degradation I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="GLUTAMINDEG-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: GLN| is the amino group donor for many cellular biosynthetic reactions and serves as a storage reservoir of ammonia, playing an important role in nitrogen metabolism. In eukaryotes, |FRAME: GLN| is converted to |FRAME: GLT| by glutaminase, the enzyme responsible for |FRAME: GLN| catabolism. In plants, bacteria, and archaea, |FRAME: GLN| can also be converted to |FRAME: GLT| by the reversible glutamate synthase reaction, also known as GOGAT for glutamine: &amp;alpha;-oxoglutarate aminotransferase (see pathway |FRAME: GLUTAMINEFUM-PWY|) |CITS: [VOET04] [GOTTSCHALK1986]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Glutaminases are present in both eukaryotes and prokaryotes and play important roles in nitrogen metabolism by effecting glutamine breakdown. Many microorgansisms can grow with |FRAME: GLN| as the sole source of carbon and nitrogen. The catabolism of glutamine in mammals is initiated by mitochondrial glutaminases. Mammals typically express two forms of mitochondrial glutaminase. The liver type glutaminase is expressed only in adult liver tissue, while the kidney type glutaminase is expressed in kidney, brain, intestine, immune system cells, and in many transformed cells, as well as fetal liver cells |CITS:[11130979]| Eukaryotic glutaminases differ in properties from those of prokaryotes. The mammalian enzyme is encoded by a single gene and expressed as a proenzyme with a mitochondrial targeting signal |CITS:[12963351]|. Upon entering the mitochondrion the protein is processed into two different forms. Upon activation by phosphate, the two subunits form a heterotetramer, composed of one large subunit and three of the smaller subnits |CITS:[2211660][7836378]|. Most prokaryotic enzymes are different, forming a regular homodimer, although a |FRAME: CPLX-7345 &quot;phosphate-activated glutaminase&quot;| has been characterized from the bacterium |FRAME: TAX-1474| |CITS: [11786258]|. Two enzymes able to degrade |FRAME: GLN|, |FRAME: GLUTAMINA-CPLX| and |FRAME: GLUTAMINB-CPLX|, were purified from |FRAME: TAX-413997|; no mutants were identified at the time, and thus the identity of the genes encoding them has not been established. Recently, two genes encoding glutaminases with properties resembling those of glutaminase A and B have been identified in |FRAME: TAX-83333| |CITS: [18459799]|. Reviewed in Reitzer, L., Catabolism of Amino Acids and Related Compounds, Module 3.4.7 |CITS: [ecosal]|.</Description>
